<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      18b. Two unrelated repairs from the authorization audit, kept in one changeset because both
      are catalogue corrections and neither is large enough to justify its own file.

      ══ 1. file.* — the permissions file-service never had ══

      file-service shipped five endpoints (upload, list, download, delete, quota) behind
      `.authenticated()` and NOTHING else: zero @PreAuthorize, zero OPA, and no file.* code
      anywhere in the 65-permission catalogue. Any authenticated token in the tenant could delete
      any file in it — a KITCHEN_STAFF token holding exactly two KDS permissions could DELETE
      /api/v1/files/{id} and succeed. That is not a gap in a gate, it is the absence of one.

      ── Granularity ──

      Three codes, not one. `file.view` and `file.upload` separate reading from writing, because
      the roles that need to attach a document to a purchase order are not the roles that should
      be able to enumerate every document in the tenant. `file.manage` is deletion, kept apart from
      upload deliberately: delete is the only irreversible operation of the five, and bundling it
      into `file.upload` would have handed it to every role that attaches an invoice scan.

      ── Who holds them ──

      OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN hold all three. MANAGER holds view + upload + manage — a branch
      manager already approves expenses and POs, and the documents backing them are the same
      documents. ACCOUNTANT and INVENTORY_MANAGER hold view + upload: both attach supporting
      documents (invoices, GRN photos) and neither has any reason to delete one. CASHIER, WAITER
      and KITCHEN_STAFF hold none — none of the three has a file workflow, and the previous state
      where all of them could delete anything is precisely what this repairs.

      Granted EXPLICITLY, never through a catalogue-wide SELECT. Every blanket grant in this
      changelog (030/041/042/045/046/057) is point-in-time — it enumerates what existed the day it
      ran and retro-grants nothing added later. 055 and 059 both say this and both grant explicitly
      for the same reason. A permission no role holds produces a clean, confident 403 for every
      user including OWNER, which is the defect class PermissionCatalogClosureTest exists to catch.

      ══ 2. FINANCE_VIEWER — a role_code with grants and no role ══

      `role_permissions` carried two FINANCE_VIEWER rows (finance.coa.view from 037, hr.payroll.view
      from 045) while `roles` had no FINANCE_VIEWER row at all. Verified live before this changeset:

        auth_db=# SELECT code FROM roles;
         OWNER, TENANT_ADMIN, MANAGER, ACCOUNTANT, INVENTORY_MANAGER, CASHIER, KITCHEN_STAFF, WAITER
        auth_db=# SELECT permission_code FROM role_permissions WHERE role_code='FINANCE_VIEWER';
         finance.coa.view
         hr.payroll.view

      ── How it drifted ──

      NOT a context problem — 035 carries context="seed" and that context demonstrably ran here.
      Verified in databasechangelog:

        auth_db=# SELECT id, contexts, dateexecuted FROM databasechangelog WHERE id LIKE '%035%';
         auth-1.0.0-035-seed-system-roles | seed | 2026-06-24 00:34:12

      035 ran on 2026-06-24 with the six roles it declared THEN. The FINANCE_VIEWER row was added to
      it on 2026-06-27, in commit b63074a. runOnChange="false" means that edit never re-applied, so
      every already-migrated database keeps the original six forever, while 037 and 045 — running
      later — grant to the seventh regardless.

      This is exactly the mechanism 057 documents one table over, and its consequence is the same:
      a fresh database and an existing one disagree permanently, and nothing complains. The role is
      unassignable (RoleCatalog.requireKnown rejects any code with no `roles` row) and no user holds
      it, so nothing is broken for a user today; what is broken is that the grant table describes a
      role the role table does not have, and anything reasoning over the grant lattice reads two
      permissions belonging to nobody.

      No repository-reading test can catch this, because the repository is correct — 030/035 declare
      FINANCE_VIEWER properly. Only the deployed rows are wrong, which is why the repair has to be a
      migration, and why it verifies itself below rather than trusting the DELETE.

      ── Delete rather than create ──

      035's own comment calls FINANCE_VIEWER "Dev-only". It exists to hold view+post journal
      entries without triggering the TOTP step-up, i.e. as a convenience for local testing, and
      ACCOUNTANT covers the real capability. Creating the role in every production database to
      satisfy a foreign-key-shaped invariant would ship a dev affordance to customers; deleting the
      orphan grants states the truth, which is that the role is seed-only. The `roles` row still
      appears on seeded dev databases via 035, and this changeset deliberately does NOT remove it
      there.

      RoleCatalogClosureTest (added with this changeset) covers the OTHER half of this defect
      class: a grant naming a role the changelog never declares at all. It cannot see the case
      repaired here — see above — and its javadoc says so rather than implying coverage it does not
      have. PermissionCatalogClosureTest could never have seen either: the codes granted are both
      real; it is the ROLE that does not exist.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-082-file-permissions" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>file.view / file.upload / file.manage — file-service had no permission at all</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            INSERT INTO permissions (code, module, description) VALUES
              ('file.view',   'file', 'List file metadata and download file content for the tenant'),
              ('file.upload', 'file', 'Upload a file to the tenant''s storage'),
              ('file.manage', 'file', 'Delete a tenant file (soft-delete; irreversible from the API)')
            ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('OWNER',             'file.view'),
              ('OWNER',             'file.upload'),
              ('OWNER',             'file.manage'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN',      'file.view'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN',      'file.upload'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN',      'file.manage'),
              ('MANAGER',           'file.view'),
              ('MANAGER',           'file.upload'),
              ('MANAGER',           'file.manage'),
              ('ACCOUNTANT',        'file.view'),
              ('ACCOUNTANT',        'file.upload'),
              ('INVENTORY_MANAGER', 'file.view'),
              ('INVENTORY_MANAGER', 'file.upload')
            ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE permission_code IN ('file.view', 'file.upload', 'file.manage');
                DELETE FROM permissions WHERE code IN ('file.view', 'file.upload', 'file.manage');
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-082-drop-orphan-finance-viewer-grants" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>Remove role_permissions rows for FINANCE_VIEWER where the role itself does not exist</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            DELETE FROM role_permissions rp
             WHERE rp.role_code = 'FINANCE_VIEWER'
               AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM roles r WHERE r.code = rp.role_code);
        </sql>

        <!--
          Say so if it did not converge, rather than leaving a grant lattice that quietly disagrees
          with itself. Same reasoning as 056's and 057's RAISE: an operator should be told, not left
          to deduce it from a role picker coming up short three weeks later.

          Checks EVERY role_code, not just FINANCE_VIEWER — the point of a repair changeset is to
          leave the invariant true, and a second orphan introduced by some other route should stop
          the migration rather than ride along behind the one this changeset was written for.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            DECLARE orphan_roles TEXT;
            BEGIN
                SELECT string_agg(DISTINCT rp.role_code, ', ') INTO orphan_roles
                  FROM role_permissions rp
                 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM roles r WHERE r.code = rp.role_code);
                IF orphan_roles IS NOT NULL THEN
                    RAISE EXCEPTION
                      'role_permissions still grants to role(s) that do not exist in roles: %. A grant to a non-existent role is invisible at runtime - nobody holds the role, so nobody is refused anything - but every consumer that reasons over the grant lattice (the 13-07 role-catalogue ceiling in particular) reads permissions belonging to nobody. Either add the roles row or delete the grants.',
                      orphan_roles;
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>

        <!--
          Guarded by NOT EXISTS so this is a no-op on a seeded dev database where 035 did create the
          role: there the grants are legitimate and must stay. It only removes rows describing a
          role that is genuinely absent.
        -->
        <rollback>
            <!--
              No rollback. Reversing this means re-inserting grants for a role that does not exist,
              which is the defect. 054 and 057 make the same call: a migration's job is not to
              restore broken data. If FINANCE_VIEWER is later wanted in production, add the `roles`
              row in a new changeset without the seed context and re-grant deliberately.
            -->
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
